<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#temple">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as templum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A temple is a place of worship, a building used for rituals such as prayer and sacrifice. The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> templum: in Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a sacred precinct marked out on the ground by a priest, or augur. By convention English uses the word for the places of worship of most ancient religions, such as the Ancient Egyptian and Ancient Greek religions, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> and others, but not usually for Christian churches, mosques or synagogues. Believers often regard a temple as the house of one or more deities, where offerings are made and a group of clergy keeps the rituals, and parts of it, or the whole main building, may be open to the clergy alone.</p></div>
